---
title: Shepherd's Pie
description: Classic comfort food
servings: 6
course: Main
locale: en_GB
prep time: 20m
cook time: 2h
difficulty: Hard
cuisine: British
tags:
- savoury
- meat
- main-course
- british
---

Heat the @beef (or lamb) dripping{2%tbsp} in a #large frying pan{} over a medium-high heat. Fry the @minced lamb{800%g}(or goat/hogget/mutton, not too lean) in batches until crisp and well browned all over (not just beige). Transfer the browned meat into a bowl and repeat with the rest.

> This is likely to create a mess!

Turn down the heat, add the @onion{1%large}(thinly sliced) to the pan, and cook until it begins to soften. Add the @carrots{2%large}(peeled and diced), @celery{3%sticks}(diced), @rosemary{2%sprigs}(leaves picked and roughly chopped) and @thyme{2%sprigs}(leaves picked and roughly chopped), and cook until tender but not soft. Sprinkle over the @flour{1%tbsp} and stir well to distribute. Add the @lamb (or beef) stock{300%ml}, @Worcestershire sauce{2%tbsp}, and @tomato purée{1%tbsp}. Scrape the bottom of the pan to dislodge any stuck bits.

Return the lamb to the pan and bring everything to a simmer. Cover, turn down the heat, and simmer for ~{45%minutes}. Remove the lid and simmer uncovered for a further ~{15%minutes}. Set aside to cool.

While the meat is cooking, peel the @floury potatoes{1.5%kg}, and cut them into evenly sized chunks. Boil in well-salted water until tender. Drain well and return to the hot pan to steam dry. Mash with the @butter{100%g} and @whole milk{1%tbsp}. Season with @salt, @pepper, and @nutmeg{}(to grate).

Heat the oven to 180°C.

Spoon the meat mix into a #30cm × 20cm ovenproof dish{}, then cover with the mash. Smooth out the top, then use a fork to make furrows over the top. Bake for about ~{30%minutes}, until lightly golden on top. Serve with greens.